{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 28, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during a turbulent era marked by rising Islamic power under Saladin. His early life involved captivity after the Battle of Inab where he spent roughly ten years as a prisoner in Aleppo before gaining freedom through a ransom payment. This long imprisonment shaped his worldview and made him deeply cautious about engaging in reckless military campaigns against Muslim forces. He became known for his pragmatic approach to diplomacy and his willingness to negotiate truces rather than pursue constant warfare which often put him at odds with more aggressive factions within the Crusader states.\n\n*Early Life and Captivity*\nRaymond assumed his title while still a young man following the assassination of his father Raymond II. The young count faced immediate challenges from local assassins and external enemies who sought to exploit the leadership transition. His capture by Nur ad-Din Zengi removed him from political life for a decade yet this period allowed him to learn Arabic and understand Muslim culture in ways that few other Crusader nobles ever achieved. This unique perspective informed his later policies and made him a valuable but sometimes controversial advisor to the kings of Jerusalem.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nKing Baldwin IV appointed Raymond as regent due to the leprosy affecting the young monarch and the need for stable leadership. Raymond managed state affairs with competence though he faced opposition from the Courtenay family and other noble houses who distrusted his conciliatory stance toward Muslim neighbors. He arranged the marriage of Princess Sibylla to William of Montferrat in an attempt to secure alliances yet internal court intrigue constantly undermined his authority. His relationship with Guy of Lusignan remained particularly hostile as Guy represented the warlike faction that Raymond believed would lead the kingdom to ruin.\n\n*The Road to Hattin*\nTensions reached a breaking point when Raynald of Châtillon attacked a Muslim caravan and violated existing peace treaties. Raymond advised caution but King Guy listened to warmer advice and marched the Christian army into the desert where they suffered a catastrophic defeat at the Horns of Hattin. Raymond escaped the battle with a small contingent while most of the Crusader nobility perished or faced capture. This disaster opened the path for Saladin to conquer Jerusalem and nearly erase the Crusader presence in the Holy Land. Raymond died shortly after these events possibly from illness or despair over the collapse of the kingdom he tried to save through diplomacy and careful strategy.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -2.8253e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0024328595}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.019564148}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.027731227}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -2.5033983e-06}, {"token": "2", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -2.384186e-07}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -2.1457695e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.0007236834}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.069994725}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"toke